<p>Puerto de Mogán is often called the "Little Venice of Gran Canaria," a nickname that comes from the small canals running through the resort, crossed by a series of footbridges that link the pedestrianised lanes on either side. Low-rise buildings in white, ochre, and pastel tones line the waterways, their balconies and walls heavy with bougainvillea in shades of pink, purple, and orange for much of the year. It's a compact, walkable place, built at a human scale that's noticeably different from the taller hotel developments found elsewhere on the island's south coast.</p>
